I love massively multiplayer  role-playing games  I love Elder Scrolls games and I love  exploring huge fantasy worlds yet it was  weird over the last year or so the Elder  Scrolls online didn't get me excited it  looked old and it played old this past  weekend I got to play a few dozen hours  of it I still think it feels old and  it's clearly torn between two worlds the  world of the MMO and the world of Elder  Scrolls but there's just enough here to  make me look forward to what the final  release will have to offer what drew me  in was the world yes it's a world many  of us already know but the Elder Scrolls  online uses that familiarity to its  advantage easing back on the user  interface and allowing Tamriel to fill  the screen by default you don't see  names hovering over the heads of your  fellow players and the hotbar containing  your attack icons disappears when you're  not engaged in combat as you stroll  about the streets of Daggerfall the  compass at the top of the screen and a  brief quest description are your only  on-screen guides this is where the game  excels the series high fantasy tone is  front and center creepy graveyards and  tranquil meadows both have their unique  kind of beauty this is a place I want to  be as I played however it didn't take me  long to see the downside of the game's  focus on immersion and it didn't help  that the group of folks playing was  relatively small I was approaching the  Elder Scrolls online like Skyrim or  Oblivion as a solo adventure I  occasionally noticed other players but  there was no reason to interact with  them so I had to ask myself does this  game need to be online at all I'm sure  it's too soon to know however because I  was a lone adventurer it didn't take  long for me to notice that next to say  Skyrim the Elder Scrolls online wasn't  stacking up the usual Elder Scrolls  invitation to go anywhere and do  anything has been stifled a bit  instead sending you down a path more  akin to other online RPGs nevertheless I  enjoyed my questing time Bethesda has  always done a great job of taking  typical RPG duties and giving them good  context so that you don't feel like  you're repeating the same activities  over and over again when villagers were  burnt out of their homes I went in  search of stray victims in need of  assistance when I was transported to the  Past I discovered the origins of a  racial conflict that had present-day  implications and in many of those cases  I had choices on how to proceed for  example I could interrogate prisoners so  that they would divulge the information  I required or I could order the guards  to slit their throats and then compel  the victims spirits to spill their  ghostly guts of course a lot of these  quests required that I slash up bandits  and wolves with daggers or fling fire at  them I liked that the Elder Scrolls  online provides so much flexibility in  how you develop your character  sadly the clunky combat just doesn't  measure up the stiff animations and  seemingly random hit boxes make for a  milquetoast skirmishes but you know I  had fun in spite of the obvious identity  issues in the bland action fantasy  ambience and the varied quests kept me  absorbed the question is whether the  enjoyment can last and I'm not sure that  the Elder Scrolls online can properly  combine online adventuring and Elder  Scrolls world building without making  both aspects of the game feel incomplete  I feel mildly disappointed but I leave  it up to you  you
 

